Every folder can hold one module. A module can have up to three special root files, and each one has one job.

### Three jobs, three files

- `@mod.moth` publishes the folder's normal API.
- `@package.moth` publishes the folder's support API. At the project root it publishes the project's external package API.
- `@entry.moth` runs the page or application code.

A folder may have one, two or all three files. A folder with only `@entry.moth` is a normal module that has no API to import.

Other helper files in the folder hold shared code. All roots can bind that shared code.

### The names are fixed

Only those three names work. The compiler rejects any other `@*.moth` or `+*.moth` name and suggests a fixed root.

A `+` prefix on another source file, such as `+notes.mtf`, is also reserved by the retired support-marker rule, so that file cannot be bound. Rename it without the `+`.

`config.moth` stays the build config file. `@config.moth` is not a root and is rejected.

### Roots keep their declarations private

A root file can read its own top-level declarations and shared helper declarations. It cannot read a declaration written in another root file. Helper files cannot read declarations written in a root file either.

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    site/
    ├── @mod.moth
    ├── @package.moth
    ├── @entry.moth
    └── shared.moth
]

All three roots may bind `shared.moth`. None of them can read another root's private declarations. Move shared declarations into `shared.moth` instead.

### What each root allows

`@mod.moth` and `@package.moth` hold declarations and functions. They may export an API. They cannot hold page runtime code, page fragments or builder metadata.

`@entry.moth` holds page runtime code, page fragments and builder metadata. It also owns the page's entry point. It cannot export an API.

An imported module never runs its entry. The build command runs only selected entries.

### Names come from folders

A root filename never becomes an import name. `pages/@mod.moth` is imported as `@pages`, not `@mod`.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    @pages page_heading
]

The filename is a role marker, not a dependency path.

### Concepts related to module roots

- For the API surface see @#public-api (Public API).
- For runtime code and fragments see @#entry-runtime-and-fragments (Entry runtime and fragments).
- For page routes see @#html-routing-and-artifacts (HTML routes and artefacts).
